Z||000000||Philphine||10-07-2001||07:50 AM||||i'm wondering about direct sun damage to equipment hidden under the covers.<P>just about finished the pods for my front speakers in my '86 (in but a little more work needed), so for now the 4x10 spot is empty from where i ran my wire up to the spot to wire them to the factory wiring temporarily.<P> in my '87 i put the passive x-overs there for my components and have had no probs with them. for this car on the passenger side i know i'll be putting a small amp for rear speakers if i put in some. i have one that will fit there. on the driver side i'm thinking of trying to put in a small eq or eq face (if i got a rf symmetry or pioneer 9200). if so, do you think i'd need to put a thin piece of wood under the grill to cut down on direct sun light or should the grill cloth be enough?<P>anyone else put anything besides a speaker there? <P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||10-07-2001||08:23 AM||||Grille cloth ought to be plenty.  If you still have one of your dash grilles covered, hold it up to some bright sunlight sometime.  Not much light gets through that.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||10-08-2001||09:38 PM||||got an idea.<P>pulled out a denon 5cd changer (like fierosound has) that i've had for years. i can never get it in a car in a way that satisfies me. stuck it all over the car interior (i don't want it in the trunk) and finally it occured to me to try an slide it into one of the speaker slots.<P>didn't work. <P>it was too long and it's not made to be mounted at more than 30deg from horizontal.<P>but, it looks like one of the small 6 disc changers (alpine, clarion, panasonic that i know of) might. i've got a broken clarion changer at home that i'm going to size it up with and if it looks doable that's what i'll try. it even looks like if i got the smallest of what's available of that type it might even fit on the drivers side.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 10-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||10-11-2001||10:57 PM||||i think it will work. i couldn't find the clarion changer so i went by a local alpine dealer to get their 6disc changer dementions 'cause they advertise having the smallest. it's 10"x2.5"x6", which i found out is no longer smallest, but i used the numbers anyway to cut out a block of wood to work with.<P> i cut away every thing under the grill including the holes for the grill mounting studs and ground the studs off the grill. it still sits in position well but nothing is holding it there.i'll work that out later. i couldn't get the block in. then i cut away 1/4" from the width to simulate a pioneer changer i got numbers from. 9 3/4"x2 5/8"x7". a little deeper but i didn't have more wood. it slid in but stuck out just enough to keep the grill from sitting flush.<P>it will have to sit at an angle so i don't know if i can use an indash changer, plus those usually cost more. now i'll be looking in some of the local shops getting more numbers and trying actual changers in the space (an alpine may still fit 'cause my wood block was pretty rough). i don't really want to let the brand i use depend on what fits but i'm interested in the idea now.  <P> ||||reg||1
